Z||000000||Sootah||10-13-2001||08:04 PM||||I threw a rod on my Black 85 GT that I bought just this last Wednesday!!! I KNEW the deal was too good to be true!! Got it for $800 looks perfect ran great (Till I got on the freeway and developed the rod knock). I think the guy that had it before me was gonna fix it all up, went to change the oil, saw tons of metal and shìt in it and decided to sell it as quick as possible.<P>How much is it going to cost me to fix?? What engines will go in the Fiero w/ no mods? Can you still get this engine from GM? How much would a low mileage one cost? And if I can't put it in myself then how much is it going to cost me to have put in?<P>Crap I'm depressed. Too good to be true...<P>------------------<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sootah__Nuclear_Happy_Face_Sig.jpg">     <BR><sup><I>Never pull your pants down in Cosco, the dang monkeys will getcha</I></sup> <BR><sup><B>AIM</B>: KSSouter</sup>||||reg||8
Z||000001||Mach10||10-13-2001||08:10 PM||||Pull the pan, drop the offending cylinder... Voila! The worlds first OFFICIAL 2.33L V-5 motor  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P><BR>Tough break. Grab yourself a wrecker short-block and start swapping peices...<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000002||JohnF||10-13-2001||08:17 PM||||Welcome to the club.  <P>Only difference is that I KNEW a rod bearing was bad before I drove it home!  You can rebuild the bottom end without removing engine if crank is not damaged (stops noise for the short run, but not recommended by the real re-builders).  Just check this forum for the subject.  Lay on your back and get with it!  You can change engines - removed from existing car - $400 and up.  Rebuilt 2.8 (then 2.9) from Fiero Factory about $1400 on your driveway (1200 + core + shipping) with a good warranty.  New 2.8 from GM - don't even think about it!  <P>Installed - you can do it for about $150 or you can pay $600 to $900 for the service.<P>Your choice!  <P>Sorry for the bad news.  Good news is that you have a relatively good car, it seems, for $800 initial investment.<P>Just another $2000 and you have a great running car!||||reg||9
Z||000003||SKIDMARK||10-13-2001||09:34 PM||||Sounds like a prime candidate for a V-8. If not then get yourself a 2.8 or 3.1 short block and start swapping parts. If you were in the Cleveland area, I could direct you to  2 or 3 parts cars with useable engines. Salvage yards will charge about $250 to $600 for a Fiero 2.8 depending on if they know what they have or not. You can also put your parts on a 2.8 from any mid '80s FWD GM. <P>Good luck & don't crush 'em, restor 'em!<P>------------------<BR>White '85 4cyl coupe<BR>Black '86 v-6 SE<BR>'81 CB900F Super Sport||||reg||3
Z||000004||85ZainoGT||10-13-2001||09:37 PM||||you can buy my 85 gt for $2200  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> no engine problems  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Paul Ventimiglia<BR>1985 White/Silver GT <BR>2.8L 4 Speed LOADED :)<BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Sigpic2529_1.jpg"> ||||reg||14
Z||000005||FieroRacer||10-14-2001||12:03 AM||||Local GM wanted 2500 for a 2.8L block for my 85 GT while stating that engine is no longer available new.  2500 for parts alone for a rebuilt is bull **** .  If you want to keep your original block (providing no other damage), look into a 3.1 stroker kit.  They run about $460 for new parts.||||reg||1
Z||000006||DreXteR||10-14-2001||12:53 AM||||Easy!!  U can fix it with only $1!!!<P>Get $1 off premium gas and burn it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smiley_damon.gif"><P>heeheh j/k   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>If the car have many miles rebuild it if not only fix the damage and start collecting money for the big fix.<P>Luck<P>DreX<p>[This message has been edited by DreXteR (edited 10-14-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000007||Kasdan||10-14-2001||01:00 AM||||heh, i did the same thing, same exact thing.  i threw a rod, bought a new long block for 1300 with a 30k warranty.  it goes in monday, i cant wait.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Linx||10-14-2001||01:42 AM||||Well- it can be done.  I did it; replaced only the crank (and bearings) and never removed the engine.  After I removed the flex-plate-to-torque-converter bolts, I separated the block from the tranny...moved it over a few inches...and dropped and replaced the crank.<P>I ran it that way for a year and never had a problem.  Then one day, I bought a compression gauge..but that's another story all together.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>In any case...it CAN be done.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Fformula88||10-14-2001||11:38 AM||||It smells like its time for a 3.4L crate engine from GM.  Its brand new and should last for as long as you will ever need it to, it uses all your current Fiero bolt ons (accessories like Alt, intake, exhaust, electrical stuff etc etc.)  You will just need some new starter holes drilled into it and your good to go!||||reg||1
Z||000010||Sootah||10-14-2001||06:59 PM||||How much would a short block rebuild cost? I've actually got a 2.8 in my backyard. Been sitting for more than 3 years though. Oil cap is missing. What I plan on right now would be to have that block rebuilt and to use all the components from my GT on the SE's block. W/ the whole engine being put into the GT, of course.<P>How much would that cost me?||||reg||1
Z||000011||Sootah||10-14-2001||07:00 PM||||Kadsan: Where did you find your long block? May go that way if it's easier/cheaper.||||reg||1
