Z||000013||Kasdan||10-14-2001||10:06 PM||||i got mine from a local company in Athens Georgia, i forgot the name.  If i were you, id get one from Jasper Engines, their NASCAR  certified and stuff, great motors and tranny rebuilders.||||reg||1
Z||000014||88Style||10-14-2001||10:10 PM||||I don't know about the earlier models but I wouldn't be afraid to pull the engine out of the car. I'm about to do it for the second time in a month. Why? well, vote isn't in yet but most likely because I am stupid and installed the clutch wrong. It takes a little bit of creativity and a willingness to get very banged up and very dirty but all in all, it's not that bad! I am rather new to Fieros but I have read an awful lot about them and from what I have read you can swap any other 60* V6 motor into the car with little or no modifications. Any FWD 2.8lt V6 of the same year will fit and I believe all others will fit with the modification of the starter location. If you have the tools/workspace/patience, this would be the way I would go...<P>||||reg||1
Z||000015||SLYGUY||10-14-2001||11:44 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 88Style:<BR><B>I don't know about the earlier models but I wouldn't be afraid to pull the engine out of the car. I'm about to do it for the second time in a month. Why? well, vote isn't in yet but most likely because I am stupid and installed the clutch wrong. It takes a little bit of creativity and a willingness to get very banged up and very dirty but all in all, it's not that bad! I am rather new to Fieros but I have read an awful lot about them and from what I have read you can swap any other 60* V6 motor into the car with little or no modifications. Any FWD 2.8lt V6 of the same year will fit and I believe all others will fit with the modification of the starter location. If you have the tools/workspace/patience, this would be the way I would go...<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Last time I checked, you needed a VEHICLE HOIST. How would you drop the motor and tranny from the car without raising it in the air?<BR>You would have a hell of a time squeezing one into a tool box too.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Sly||||reg||1
Z||000016||DJRice||10-14-2001||11:51 PM||||Vehicle hoist #1.= 2 floor jacks, 2 jack stands, 2 wheel chocks, 8 concrete blocks, and 1 Pressure treated 4x4x8.  (it takes a while but it can be done)<P>Vehicle hoist #2.  Engine hoist, chain.||||reg||1
Z||000017||roadwarrior||10-15-2001||11:45 PM||||bull sh*t you need a hoist, jack the car up and block it where the rear frame rails join the floor then disconect everything from then engine and lower it using either blocks(for lowering it one side at a time) or two floor jacks by placing one on each side. viola<P>||||reg||1
Z||000018||DJRice||10-16-2001||09:20 PM||||I meant either #1 or #2.  ||||reg||1
Z||000019||FieroPerformance||10-16-2001||09:25 PM||||I am rebuilding my engine. Complete rebuild. Reconditioned block, heads, and crank. New higher compression power forged pistons, high performance cam, roller tip rockers ect.<P>Right now I am at about $1,500 and still spending.<P>It is expensive as hell but I am sure it will be worth the wait, work, blood, sweat, tears and money.<P>-Charlie<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic.jpg"> ||||reg||1
